      "feedback": "The Joannis Palm (Veitchia joannis) is an absolute masterpiece of historical drama and evolutionary engineering. It is the definitive \"type species\" that anchors its entire genus, and it carries a tragic Victorian backstory that will have Marie Selby curators immediately leaning in.\n\nBecause Bradenton sits right at the razor-thin northern edge of where this tropical Fijian giant can survive outdoors, its presence at Palma Sola is a badge of horticultural honor. Here is how we inject that elite, drool-worthy swagger into your Quick Hits and What to Look For fields.\n\n🌟 The \"Selby-Level\" Prose Upgrades\n1. The Victorian Tragedy (Quick Hits - Bullet 1)\nInstead of just stating the history, let's treat it like a botanical monument. Selby curators revere the golden age of plant hunting.\n\nCurrent Draft: \"The palm that gave an entire genus its name arrived in Western botany because a young Victorian plant hunter visited Fiji in the 1860s... John Gould Veitch died of tuberculosis at 31...\"\n\nThe D",
